Maya: heads up — opened a docs PR to replace old streaming examples with the async-iterator pattern for Python and a small TS event-fragment parser. PR: https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/497 :party:

docs-bot: PR created by @Maya — reviewers requested: @Eli, @Sam — checks queued

Eli: thanks. can you drop a short before/after snippet for Python here? i want to make sure we call out the `max_concurrency` caveat.

Maya: sure — before (old sync streaming):

```python
resp = client.stream_generate(model="rwd/large", prompt="...")
for chunk in resp.chunks():
    print(chunk.text, end="")
```

After (async iterator pattern):

```python
async for chunk in client.stream_generate_async(model="rwd/large", prompt="...", max_concurrency=4):
    print(chunk.text, end="")
```

Notes: mention `max_concurrency` default changed to 2 in this release — add migration note.

Sam: for TS i added an event-fragment helper to assemble partial JSON tool-calls during streaming. draft snippet: 

```ts
const stream = await client.streamGenerate({ model: 'rwd/medium', prompt });
for await (const ev of stream) {
  if (ev.type === 'event') {
    fragmentAssembler.push(ev.data);
    if (ev.complete) {
      const obj = JSON.parse(fragmentAssembler.join(''))
      handleToolCall(obj)
      fragmentAssembler.length = 0
    }
  }
}
```

Eli: can we also add a Go snippet for cursor-based pagination with exponential backoff? support asked from a few customers.

Maya: yes — i drafted one in the PR but it's small. dropping it here for quick review:

```go
ctx := context.Background()
client := sdk.NewClient(apiKey)
cursor := ""
for {
    resp, err := client.ListItems(ctx, &ListRequest{Limit: 100, Cursor: cursor})
    if err != nil {
        time.Sleep(backoff.Next()) // exponential backoff helper
        continue
    }
    for _, it := range resp.Items {
        fmt.Println(it.Id)
    }
    if resp.Cursor == "" {
        break
    }
    cursor = resp.Cursor
}
```

Sam: small nit — mention `resp.Cursor` can be opaque token and length may vary, don't assume base64. also add note on respecting rate-limits & 429 handling. I can add a snippet for 429 handling if you want.

